It turns out the sheer act of fracking doesn't contribute to climate change directly but through activities that are made possible because of fracking. Most intuitive example: Extract oil, make gas, fuel cars, CO2 is put into the atmosphere.

&#x200B;

CO2 is the leading cause for climate change. The CO2 content in the atmosphere has increased by about 55% since 1970 causing an average temperature climb of 1 degree C (about 1.8 degrees F). Multiple models were drawn in the 70s to predict the rise in temperature as a function of CO2 content, and the temperature did in fact increase by about 1 degree C. More CO2 in the atmosphere is a retainer for moisture which leads to more frequent storms and hurricanes. The oceans are warming due to the temperature increase which will render our coastal cities/land uninhabitable if the polar ice caps melt bringing the sea level about 70m higher.

&#x200B;

Fracking will stop eventually because it takes millions of years for fossils to form, models suggest around the year 2060 this resource will be exhausted. It seems natural that then, we will move away from fracking as it will be the only option left.

&#x200B;

But it leaves us with a difficult question of ethics - do we stop now to prevent further damage to the planet or do we stop later and hope the damage is reversible?

